---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import OriginalPdfEvidenceGallery from "../../components/OriginalPdfEvidenceGallery.astro";
import EvidencePanel from "../../components/EvidencePanel.astro";
const base=import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const pdf=base+"pdf-journals/NovaCorp_CR-008_File_Server_Deployment_Journal.pdf";
const page=(n:number)=>pdf+"#page="+n;
const steps=[
{title:"Check whether the domain services can be found",why:"A domain join needs DNS service discovery as well as simple IP reachability.",cmd:"Test-Connection 10.10.20.10 -Count 4\nTest-Connection 10.10.20.11 -Count 4\nResolve-DnsName _ldap._tcp.dc._msdcs.nova.corp -Type SRV",expected:"Both controllers reachable and LDAP SRV records returned.",actual:"The SRV lookup returned both DCs. ICMP could succeed while TCP/53 failed.",meaning:"A successful ping or DNS lookup did not explain the inconsistent TCP behavior."},
{title:"Inspect the actual network configuration",why:"The server and DCs should agree on which addresses are local.",cmd:'Get-NetIPAddress -InterfaceAlias "Ethernet" -AddressFamily IPv4 |\n  Format-Table IPAddress,PrefixLength,InterfaceAlias\nGet-NetRoute -AddressFamily IPv4 |\n  Sort-Object DestinationPrefix |\n  Format-Table DestinationPrefix,NextHop,InterfaceAlias,RouteMetric',expected:"All three servers use /24 with a connected 10.10.20.0/24 route.",actual:"DC01 and DC02 had /32 and lacked the connected /24 route; FILES01 had /24.",meaning:"The endpoints disagreed on how to reach each other."},
{title:"Observe packets instead of guessing",why:"Confirm whether a DNS service is unresponsive or its response takes an unexpected path.",cmd:"tcpdump -eni vmbr1 'host 10.10.20.20 and host 10.10.20.11 and tcp port 53'\nbridge fdb show br vmbr1 | grep -iE '48:1c:07|fc:06:57|4d:46:68'",expected:"SYN and SYN-ACK travel directly between hosts on the same VLAN.",actual:"SYN went directly to DC02; SYN-ACK was addressed to the pfSense MAC. Repeated retransmissions preceded a reset.",meaning:"The service was responding. The return path was wrong."},
{title:"Test the hypothesis",why:"Determine whether consistent subnet assumptions restore connectivity.",cmd:"",expected:"A temporary matching /32 configuration should route both directions consistently through the gateway.",actual:"Temporarily changing FILES01 to /32 made the connection work.",meaning:"This strengthened the diagnosis. It was an experiment, not the desired production configuration."},
{title:"Verify the final repair",why:"Confirm that correcting addressing restores multiple AD service ports, not only ping.",cmd:"Test-NetConnection 10.10.20.10 -Port 53\nTest-NetConnection 10.10.20.11 -Port 53\nTest-NetConnection 10.10.20.10 -Port 88\nTest-NetConnection 10.10.20.10 -Port 389\nTest-NetConnection 10.10.20.10 -Port 445",expected:"TCP connections succeed after the /24 correction.",actual:"The journal records successful application-port connectivity after the correction.",meaning:"The intended direct same-subnet path was restored."}
];
---
<BaseLayout title="The same network, two different subnet masks" description="An unexpected CR-008 subnet-prefix mismatch interrupted file server to domain controller communication." breadcrumbs={[{label:"Investigations",href:"incidents/"},{label:"CR-008 subnet mismatch"}]}>
<section class="page-head"><div class="container"><div class="eyebrow">Unexpected incident · CR-008 · September 2026</div><h1>The same network, two different subnet masks.</h1><p class="lead">A new file server could sometimes reach the domain controllers, but important network services failed. The reason was not a broken DNS service: the two controllers had incorrect subnet prefixes.</p></div></section>
<section class="section compact"><div class="container grid-main"><article class="prose">
<h2>What happened and why it mattered</h2><p>While deploying NOVA-FILES01, some ping and name-resolution tests worked, yet TCP/53 to a domain controller could fail. An employee-facing file service depends on reliable identity and DNS, so the baseline could not be accepted until the network was consistent. No production employee outage is claimed.</p>
<h2>The network problem in plain English</h2><p>All three machines were on the same virtual network. FILES01 knew that the controllers were local neighbors. The controllers, configured with /32, believed the file server was outside their local network and sent responses toward the router instead.</p>
<figure><svg viewBox="0 0 320 190" role="img" aria-label="Concept diagram: direct request from FILES01 to DC02, return response sent toward pfSense rather than directly to FILES01" style="width:100%;height:auto"><g fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"><rect x="8" y="12" width="130" height="50" rx="6"/><rect x="182" y="12" width="130" height="50" rx="6"/><rect x="95" y="132" width="130" height="46" rx="6"/><path d="M138 37H180"/><path d="m172 32 8 5-8 5"/><path d="M248 62 182 131" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/><path d="m184 122-2 9 9-2"/></g><g fill="currentColor" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle"><text x="73" y="30">FILES01 /24</text><text x="73" y="46">10.10.20.20</text><text x="247" y="30">DC02 /32</text><text x="247" y="46">10.10.20.11</text><text x="160" y="27">SYN</text><text x="255" y="105">SYN-ACK</text><text x="160" y="150">pfSense</text><text x="160" y="165">Unexpected return path</text></g></svg><figcaption>Explanatory diagram based on CR-008 §6.5, not a captured packet trace.</figcaption></figure>
<h2>How I investigated it</h2>
{steps.map((step,i)=><section><h3>{i+1}. {step.title}</h3><p><strong>Why:</strong> {step.why}</p>{step.cmd&&<pre><code>{step.cmd}</code></pre>}<p><strong>Expected:</strong> {step.expected}</p><p><strong>Actual:</strong> {step.actual}</p><p><strong>Interpretation:</strong> {step.meaning}</p>{i===1&&<EvidencePanel subject="Subnet and route diagnostics" requested="Original before-correction prefix and route output not included as a screenshot in CR-008; the original recorded command and result are preserved above."/>}{i===2&&<p><a href={page(16)}>Original packet-capture interpretation in CR-008, page 16 →</a></p>}{i===4&&<EvidencePanel subject="Corrected subnet and application port checks" requested="Original post-correction TCP test screenshots not included as a screenshot in CR-008; recorded validation is preserved above."/>}</section>)}
<h2>Why the workstation could still work</h2><p>VLAN 40 traffic was routed through pfSense in both directions. The journal records a successful client-to-DC02 TCP/53 test even while the same-VLAN servers had inconsistent subnet settings.</p><OriginalPdfEvidenceGallery ids={["subnet-vlan40-positive"]} heading="Actual cross-VLAN positive control"/><p><a href={page(17)}>View actual CR-008 Figure 6, page 17 →</a></p>
<h2>Root cause and final fix</h2><p>DC01 and DC02 had incorrect /32 prefixes. I restored all three VLAN 20 servers to the intended /24 configuration. The exact corrective command is not printed in the retrieved journal text; no invented command is shown.</p>
<h2>Additional validation and lesson</h2><p>After the domain join and required restart, the secure-channel test returned True. Both DCs later resolved FILES01 and reached TCP/445. Always inspect prefix lengths, route tables and both directions of a connection before blaming a service or changing firewall rules.</p><pre><code>{"Test-ComputerSecureChannel -Verbose\nResolve-DnsName nova-files01\nTest-NetConnection nova-files01 -Port 445"}</code></pre><p><a href={page(18)}>Domain trust verification, page 18 →</a> · <a href={page(22)}>DC-to-file-server validation, page 22 →</a></p>
<h2>Engineering journal</h2><p><a href={page(15)}>Original CR-008 PDF, section 6 (pages 15–17) →</a></p><p><a href={base+"journals/NovaCorp_CR-008_File_Server_Deployment_Journal/"}>Complete original journal with text and screenshots →</a></p>
</article><aside><div class="sidebox"><h3>Affected systems</h3>{["nova-files01","nova-dc01","nova-dc02","nova-fw01","nova-prox01"].map(s=><p><a href={base+"systems/"+s+"/"}>{s.toUpperCase()} →</a></p>)}<p><a href={base+"learn/active-directory/"}>Active Directory →</a></p><p><a href={base+"learn/dns/"}>DNS →</a></p></div><div class="sidebox"><h3>Incident classification</h3><p>Discovered during deployment, not deliberately injected. Corrected and validated in CR-008.</p></div></aside></div></section>
</BaseLayout>